#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073437 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073820 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7d26bc9-6f89-5da4-b8a9-6be6f4280f81" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Additive Inverse of a Positive Integer"}] :question/id #uuid "735e0a05-bc90-5b18-b096-d51d9f9c50e3" :question/math-academy-id "e-12780" :question/problem "What is the additive inverse of $40$?" :question/worked-solution "To find the additive inverse of the number $40$, we change its sign by placing a minus symbol $({\\color{red}{-}})$ in front of it. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{-}}\\,(40) = -40\n$$\n\nTo check our work, we add the inverse to the original number. If we're correct, we should get zero.\n\n$$\n40+(-40) = 40-40 = 0 \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073438 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073821 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b38f30b0-9e05-510f-9ba6-928ab1b8482b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Additive Inverse of a Negative Integer"}] :question/id #uuid "de14e5df-9b12-5878-a134-9b02db1d5b5d" :question/math-academy-id "e-12781" :question/problem "What number can be added to $-28$ to get a sum of $0$?" :question/worked-solution "The number that can be added to $-28$ to give $0$ is the additive inverse of $-28$.\n\nTo find the additive inverse of the number $-28$, we change its sign by placing a minus symbol $({\\color{red}{-}})$ in front of it. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{-}}\\,(-28) = 28\n$$\n\nTo check our work, we add the inverse to the original number. If we're correct, we should get zero.\n\n$$\n(-28)+28 = -28+28= 0 \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073439 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073822 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b88b2def-7312-5076-9ff3-60319d37f1d6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Additive Inverse of a Decimal or Fraction"}] :question/id #uuid "8b152054-aec8-58f3-9a7a-e435e49fe8c9" :question/math-academy-id "e-12782" :question/problem "What is the additive inverse of $0.72$?" :question/worked-solution "To find the additive inverse of the number $0.72$, we change its sign by placing a minus symbol $({\\color{red}{-}})$ in front of it. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{-}}\\,(0.72) = -0.72\n$$\n\nTo check our work, we add the inverse to the original number. If we're correct, we should get zero.\n\n$$\n0.72+(-0.72) = 0.72-0.72 = 0 \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073440 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "327a7c63-425d-511a-b629-be5d407cbcc4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Additive Inverse on a Number Line"}] :question/id #uuid "f0a3790d-c67d-5b70-b33e-ce71f8606ad3" :question/math-academy-id "e-12783" :question/problem "How can we represent the additive inverse of $-2$ on the number line?" :question/worked-solution "The only two numbers that are at a distance of $2$ from zero are $2$ and $-2$. So, $2$ is the additive inverse of $-2$.\n\n![](images/25/259f6788e458fc902185709350128e8e8df2bcacb567eb7c5ab85f99aadc47b5.png)\n\nTherefore, the additive inverse of $-2$ is $2$, and can be represented on the number line as follows:\n\n![](images/38/384dc1354574dcd2f43ef5871421d7ca35e5e04498187f3a912c389a14941c43.png)"}]}
